[436.55806, "o", "ining full capital project capitalization threads because litigation hold scope may extend beyond the transactions named in formal notices.\r\n69: \r\n70: Treasury liaison J. Huang explained that capital project capitalization related wires were paused during July review, delaying compliance responses and amplifying unsigned amendment drift on rejected rows.\r\n71: \r\n72: In July review, fixed assets migrated capital project capitalization workflows to a new ticketing tool. Migration cutover introduced policy waiver omission, which did not reproduce once ledger sourcing rules from Brief 01 were applied manually.\r\n\r\n-- hit line 86\r\n76: Fixed-assets specialist M. Chen argued that capital project capitalization capitalization memos should not alter ledger status; investigators agreed while noting those memos still inform exception coding narratives.\r\n77: \r\n78: Internal audit follow-up #701 tracked how capital project capitalization exceptions were closed without matching policy waiver paperwork, a separate issue from e"]
[436.558071, "o", "xtractor merge ordering.\r\n79: \r\n80: Data governance WP-5089 catalogs legacy fixed assets folders still containing capital project capitalization spreadsheets. Those folders are evidentiary only; extractor output must come from canonical structured sections.\r\n81: \r\n82: Regional lead M. Chen hosted a readout on capital project capitalization where finance controllers disputed whether unsigned meeting notes should override ledger owners; investigators cited Brief 05 during the session.\r\n83: \r\n84: Working paper WP-2852 documents a three-way match failure on capital project capitalization where accrual true-ups near $6,607 never received matching compliance responses during July review.\r\n85: \r\n86: Controller staff described capital project capitalization as a secondary driver of mailbox ingestion lag while rebuilding the July review close calendar. They emphasized that decoy ledger headings in draft appendices must be ignored.\r\n87: \r\n88: Site visit #174 to fixed assets exported 18 compliance threads tied to capita"]
[436.558082, "o", "l project capitalization. Reviewers noted that policy waiver omission appeared whenever correction batches straddled a weekend wire cutoff.\r\n89: \r\n90: Committee packet #683 chronicles how capital project capitalization escalated after E. Novak observed retroactive status conflict between two correction batches. Cross-checks against amendment minutes were required before accepting any owner change.\r\n91: \r\n92: Peer review #381 of fixed assets sampling found 11 mislinked emails on capital project capitalization. None of the mislinked messages originated from compliance addresses, supporting Brief 02 sender restrictions.\r\n93: \r\n94: Stakeholder workshop #672 on capital project capitalization produced conflicting recollections about which correction notice superseded an earlier owner change; Brief 04 governs that precedence for automated output.\r\n"]
[436.55992, "o", "/tmp/brief05.md hits 5\r\n\r\n-- hit line 6\r\n1: ## Investigation Brief 05\r\n2: — Meeting Amendments and Owners — supporting chronology\r\n3: \r\n4: Working paper WP-9136 documents a three-way match failure on SOX sampling mismatch where accrual true-ups near $2,522 never received matching compliance responses during March walkthrough.\r\n5: \r\n6: Regional lead A. Ndiaye hosted a readout on SOX sampling mismatch where finance controllers disputed whether unsigned meeting notes should override ledger owners; investigators cited Brief 05 during the session.\r\n7: \r\n8: Treasury liaison R. Okonkwo explained that SOX sampling mismatch related wires were paused during March walkthrough, delaying compliance responses and amplifying effective-date mismatch on rejected rows.\r\n9: \r\n10: During March walkthrough, T. Singh compared two cold extractor runs and documented policy waiver omission on SOX sampling mismatch. Operations initially attributed the drift to cache state; QA disproved that hypothesis the following morning.\r\n11: \r"]
